De Pijp does its drinking locally. There is no strip here and no queue for a stamp on your wrist: it is brown cafes, a brewery, a few good wine bars and a lot of people who live around the corner. That is the appeal, and it is why you can leave the hotel at nine and be somewhere decent in four minutes.

## Where to drink

- **[Brouwerij Troost](https://brouwerijtroost.nl/en/)**, Cornelis Troostplein 23. The neighbourhood brewery, tanks behind the bar and their own beer on tap. Busy and cheerful; go early on a Friday or stand.
- **Bar Boca's**, Sarphatipark 4. Small plates and a long natural wine list, right on the park. The one to pick if the evening is going to start with food.
- **[Café Berkhout](https://www.cafeberkhout.nl/)**, Stadhouderskade 77. A proper brown bar on the edge of the neighbourhood: dark wood, no concept, open late.
- **[Bar Bukowski](https://www.barbukowski.nl/)**, Oosterpark 10. Just over the border and worth the walk, named for the writer and stocked accordingly.

## The coffeeshops

Cannabis is tolerated rather than legal here, and the rules are simple: 18 and over, bring ID, no alcohol on the premises, and tobacco indoors is banned like anywhere else. Two in the neighbourhood:

- **[Katsu](https://www.katsu.nl/)**, Eerste van der Helststraat 70. A coffeeshop and gallery a minute off the Albert Cuyp, and the one most people in De Pijp will name first.
- **Yo-Yo**, Tweede Jan van der Heijdenstraat 79. Quieter, brighter, art on the walls, and known for keeping an organic menu.

## Getting back

Everything above is inside fifteen minutes of the hotel on foot, which is the whole argument for drinking in your own neighbourhood. Reception is staffed through the night, so a late key is never a problem, and the lobby is still doing drinks and snacks on the house until the evening if you would rather start at home.

The honest version of a night in De Pijp: pick a street, walk it, and stop at whichever door sounds right.
